---
// About — what this site is, what it deliberately is not, and how it relates to
// the canonical field resources. Kept short and honest about provenance.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Base title="About · Body of Knowledge" active="about">
  <article class="container prose">
    <div class="eyebrow">About</div>
    <h1>A guided reader, not a replacement.</h1>

    <p>
      This site is a curated reader over the field's Learning Engineering Body of
      Knowledge. Its one job is to help people find a path into a broad and
      growing field: <a href={base + "journeys/"}>learning journeys</a> that route
      through the material for a particular starting point, and a
      <a href={base + "knowledge-map/"}>knowledge map</a> to browse by area.
    </p>

    <h2>What it is not</h2>
    <p>
      It is not the canonical corpus, and it does not duplicate it. The shared
      reading list, tools catalog, practice library, events, and community live at
      the <a href="https://lecommons.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Learning
      Engineering Commons</a>; the reference body of knowledge lives at the
      <a href="https://lebok.wiki" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LEBoK wiki</a>
      maintained through IEEE ICICLE. Every journey stop links back to that
      material rather than restating it. It is also not tied to any single degree
      program — it is a field-level resource.
    </p>

    <h2>On the taxonomy</h2>
    <p>
      The knowledge areas used to organize journeys currently follow the Learning
      Engineering Competency Framework synthesis prepared for the IEEE standards
      pathway — the closest documented field-level map available at build time.
      Before a public launch these should be reconciled against the canonical
      LEBoK knowledge areas, and adjusted where they differ.
    </p>

    <h2>Contributing a journey</h2>
    <p>
      Journeys are MDX files under <code>src/content/journeys/</code>. Each names
      an audience, an ordered list of stops, and — for every stop — the knowledge
      area it advances and onward links to canonical sources. Adding a path is a
      pull request, not a code change.
    </p>
  </article>

  <style>
    .prose h2 { margin-top: 2rem; }
    .prose p { max-width: var(--measure); }
  </style>
</Base>
